  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Married daughters have equal legal rights as sons to their father's property under the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005. Marriage does not change or remove a daughter's right to inherit her share.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005",
            "link": "https://www.scconline.com/blog/post/2026/02/03/daughters-property-rights-before-and-after-2005-amendment/"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Married daughters have equal legal rights as sons to their father's property under the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Ancestral Property (Joint Family Property)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Equal Birth Right: A daughter becomes a coparcener (joint legal heir) by birth, just like a son."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Marital Status Does Not Matter: Being married does not take away her right to the ancestral property."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Right to Demand Partition: A married daughter can ask for a division of the ancestral property and take her equal share."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Father's Status: The Supreme Court clarified in the Vineeta Sharma v. Rakesh Sharma case that a daughter has this right even if her father passed away before the 2005 amendment became active."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Self-Acquired Property (Property Bought by the Father)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "If the Father Dies Without a Will (Intestate): The married daughter gets an equal share of the property, matching the share of her brothers and mother, as a Class I legal heir.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "If the Father Makes a Valid Will (Testate): A father can choose to give his self-acquired property to anyone he wants through a legal will. If he legally excludes his daughter in a valid will, she cannot claim a share of that specific self-acquired property."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          6
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you want to discuss a specific situation, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Is the property ancestral or self-acquired?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Did the father leave a valid will?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you understand what steps to take next."
      }
    ],
